# The built UI bundle is committed, and the release workflow compares a fresh
# build against it byte for byte to catch a stale interface shipping silently.
#
# Line-ending normalization breaks that comparison. Vite writes LF on every
# platform, but a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites these files
# to CRLF, so the same bytes report as modified locally and the drift check
# stops meaning what it says -- either failing a good release or, worse, being
# routinely ignored until it misses a real one.
#
# Treated as verbatim so what is stored is what is compared, everywhere.
internal/webui/dist/** -text

# And the same for the build's inputs, which is where the drift actually came
# from. Vite copies web/public straight through without touching line endings,
# so a Windows checkout that turned these into CRLF produced a bundle a Linux
# build could never reproduce -- the release check caught it as a stale UI,
# which is exactly the wrong diagnosis but the right refusal.
web/public/** -text

# The page template, for the same reason: Vite substitutes the asset tags into
# it and writes the rest through unchanged, so its line endings end up in the
# shipped index.html. Everything else under web/src is parsed and re-emitted by
# the bundler, which normalizes on its own.
web/index.html -text
